{"name":"Lobaria Lichen","hex":"#9fc8b2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lichen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many symbiotic organisms, being associations of algae and fungi, often found as white or yellow-to-blue–green patches on rocks, old walls, etc.","Something which gradually spreads across something else, causing damage."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with lichen."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lichen"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lobaria","blocks":[{"text":"Lobaria is a genus of foliose lichens, formerly classified in the family Lobariaceae, but now placed in the Peltigeraceae. They are commonly known as \"lung wort\" or \"lungmoss\" as their physical shape somewhat resembles a lung, and their ecological niche is similar to that of moss."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Lobaria_pulmonaria_010108c.jpg/330px-Lobaria_pulmonaria_010108c.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lobaria","wikidata":"Q3501273"},{"title":"Lichen","blocks":[{"text":"A lichen is a hybrid colony of algae or cyanobacteria living symbiotically among filaments of multiple fungus species, along with bacteria embedded in the cortex, or \"skin\", in a mutualistic relationship. Lichens are the lifeform that first brought the term symbiosis into biological context."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Letharia_vulpina_JHollinger_crop.jpg/330px-Letharia_vulpina_JHollinger_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lichen","wikidata":"Q43142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Lobaria_macaronesica_C._Cornejo_%26_Scheid_407117.jpg/500px-Lobaria_macaronesica_C._Cornejo_%26_Scheid_407117.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thum